WEBVTT

00:00:05.454 --> 00:00:09.950
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Amy few years ago, Amy Malloy worked for an international media company.

00:00:10.545 --> 00:00:16.368
It was a job she wanted to do and she enjoyed the variety, but there were significant challenges.

00:00:18.154 --> 00:00:22.421
<v><b>Amy:</b></v> So, it was an office job with some other work going on,

00:00:22.485 --> 00:00:30.191
um, outside the, the office, some travel. Um, I used to develop, er, educational materials.

00:00:30.436 --> 00:00:36.086
So, there are often quite a lot of deadlines, quick deadlines, um, lots of team working.

00:00:36.451 --> 00:00:40.026
The hours weren’t crazy hours, but they,

00:00:40.193 --> 00:00:44.168
they could easily spread just from the amount of work that needed to be done.

00:00:44.946 --> 00:00:47.886
At first, I’d be…so, it was a five-day-a-week job,

00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:52.892
but I would frequently be doing probably the equivalent of six days’ work.

00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:57.412
So, I’d be doing work in the evenings to, um, just get that extra bit done,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:02.242
or I’d be starting on, um, the bus in, as well, on emails.

00:01:02.337 --> 00:01:06.849
I found that my mental health definitely started to deteriorate.

00:01:07.079 --> 00:01:15.114
And it definitely was exacerbated by, um, the amount of work that needed to be done.

00:01:15.114 --> 00:01:20.672
So, it, it definitely created anxiety around, um,

00:01:20.697 --> 00:01:24.851
racing thoughts and what came with that was just feeling, you know,

00:01:24.876 --> 00:01:26.720
lack of motivation, lack of morale.

00:01:27.228 --> 00:01:34.077
Um, and physically, I think I felt more lethargic or jumpy, you know, and I didn’t sleep as well.

00:01:34.102 --> 00:01:37.143
I’d often wake up in the middle of the night thinking there was something I needed to have done.

00:01:39.195 --> 00:01:43.308
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> This workload became increasingly unmanageable as Amy started a family.

00:01:44.657 --> 00:01:50.141
And it wasn’t long after the birth of her second child that Amy realized something had to change.

00:01:55.886 --> 00:02:02.645
<v><b>Amy:</b></v> So, I think the final decision came after I went back after my second, um, baby.

00:02:02.907 --> 00:02:07.680
I went back into a new role, um, in a different, uh, different part of the company, so,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:13.817
new boss, um, quite a senior position, and at the time I was saying, oh, this is great, you know.

00:02:13.842 --> 00:02:17.234
I’m going back into a, you know, into this role; it’s a great role.

00:02:17.456 --> 00:02:19.762
Um, but I think at the back of my mind, I always knew that

00:02:19.762 --> 00:02:24.644
it was possibly the wrong time in my life for that, you know, that role to have come.

00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:28.835
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> While Amy was on maternity leave,

00:02:29.009 --> 00:02:32.796
she had started to explore her interest in yoga and mindfulness.

00:02:33.566 --> 00:02:37.508
She completed a teacher training course and soon started to run classes

00:02:37.739 --> 00:02:42.076
– both in person and online – to help other people focus on the present

00:02:42.425 --> 00:02:45.453
and let go of their everyday stresses and anxieties.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:52.952
She soon realized she wanted to pursue this as a full-time career

00:02:52.951 --> 00:03:00.160
and started <i>No More Shoulds</i>, a social enterprise dedicated to simplifying access to yoga

00:03:00.185 --> 00:03:02.304
and mindfulness for better mental health.

00:03:06.047 --> 00:03:08.001
Alongside her normal classes,

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:12.299
Amy also does corporate and media work in mindfulness and well-being.

00:03:12.982 --> 00:03:17.923
This, combined with raising a family, means she’s still very busy a lot of the time.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:22.222
But she feels that her work–life balance has improved hugely.

00:03:24.405 --> 00:03:27.561
<v><b>Amy:</b></v> It’s much more balanced, that’s for sure.

00:03:27.648 --> 00:03:30.549
I mean, there’s always the challenges that come with starting your own business.

00:03:30.827 --> 00:03:36.063
Um, so I’d say it, it’s tougher to find the, the kind of firmer boundaries over,

00:03:36.063 --> 00:03:38.883
like, this is the time that I finish work and I leave the office,

00:03:38.883 --> 00:03:42.148
whether I carried on in the evening or not, there was that, that cut-off.

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:49.934
Um, but what I really love is that I can, I can flex everything around my family,

00:03:50.244 --> 00:03:51.960
and also around my energy.

00:03:52.078 --> 00:03:56.130
So, not only can I be there for every school pickup and drop-off, um,

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:01.621
I can also have a nap in the middle of the day if I need because just generally my energy,

00:04:01.796 --> 00:04:05.435
I have a real slump between two and three o’clock; I never achieve anything.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.415
So, I either have a nap or some quiet time or I do some reading, um,

00:04:09.542 --> 00:04:11.274
or have a cup of tea or I go for a walk.

00:04:11.544 --> 00:04:16.040
So, I feel like, whilst maybe sometimes my work might flow into an evening,

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.895
or I teach in the evenings as well, that’s actually a time when I’m more energized.

00:04:21.411 --> 00:04:26.916
So, I…it doesn’t feel like an imbalance because I can have that quiet time in the day

00:04:27.163 --> 00:04:30.720
if I need to do more quiet stuff or, you know, um,

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.830
stuff that I might have done in the evening and felt too tired to do before,

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.239
I just shift things around.

00:04:35.278 --> 00:04:39.248
So, it…the, the balance is, is brilliant right now.

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:45.420
I think we do get caught in the hamster wheel of life quite a lot.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:50.784
I think we get stuck in automatic habits and automatic patterns.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:56.831
I think there are, you know, there can be societal expectations on how you should be…

00:04:56.856 --> 00:05:02.256
how busy you should be, um, that you should say yes to everything, experience everything.

00:05:02.685 --> 00:05:06.014
So, it’s…I’d say just take a little bit of space,

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:11.065
even if it’s just a day off if you can manage that, to do nothing.

00:05:11.367 --> 00:05:15.400
And…or maybe it’s just like a little pocket of time every day or every week

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:21.849
where you just start to write down, um, little things that, that make you happy,

00:05:22.087 --> 00:05:23.868
that you haven’t done for a while,

00:05:24.074 --> 00:05:29.372
and see if you can find time to get one of those things into your week or your day,

00:05:29.809 --> 00:05:35.056
um, to really, to start to gently, gently edge a little bit more balance in.

00:05:35.222 --> 00:05:39.631
Not everybody is going to want to, or need to, or be able to make that,

00:05:39.656 --> 00:05:45.692
kind…quite seismic shift that I made. Sometimes it can be just little by little.

00:05:47.153 --> 00:05:50.404
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> And this mindful approach to work–life balance is something

00:05:50.404 --> 00:05:52.588
that many business leaders agree on, too.

00:05:53.643 --> 00:05:56.567
As the business consultant Betsy Jacobson says,

00:05:57.266 --> 00:06:01.406
‘Balance is not better time management, but better boundary management.

00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:05.522
Balance means making choices and enjoying those choices.’